{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch49y7zWfERi1zxsKw7rS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I added the invoices export and committed it as `d93e98f`: `scripts/export_invoices.py` plus the output it wrote, `out/invoices.csv` (5 of the 12 invoices). January was left alone.\n\n**Segment C is filtered out here too.** The commit that added the filter to the customers export (`3bc868b`) explains why: segment C customers declined partner sharing, and that covers \"every record we hold on them, not only this table.\" Invoices are records about those customers, so they're excluded as well.\n\n**This export can't just copy the customers filter.** The invoices table has its own `seg` column, but it doesn't match the customer's segment. Customers 103 and 105 are segment C in `tables/customers.csv`, yet their four invoices (9002, 9005, 9008, 9011) are tagged A or B. A straight copy of the customers filter would have sent those four to the partner. So the script looks up each invoice's customer in the customers table and drops segment C customers.\n\nIt also drops any invoice whose own `seg` is C (9001, 9004 and 9009). I don't know what that column means, so I erred toward leaving those three out; the commit message only mentions the customer lookup. Leaving out too much costs the partner some completeness, while sending too much would break the opt-out. If invoices with `seg` C should be included, it's a one-line change.\n\nCommits so far have used the data-month prefix only for `data/` changes, so I followed the `export_customers: …` style from the earlier script commit."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":342,"cache_read_input_tokens":20038,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":342},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},